# PART 2A - Hyperstack GPU machine + SSH + VS Code + WinSCP (connection from zero)
## BEFORE YOU START: what you are building in this part
Your laptop has no strong GPU, so you rent one. Hyperstack is a cloud company that rents computers with NVIDIA GPUs by the hour. You will: (1) install two programs on your laptop (VS Code and WinSCP), (2) make an SSH key (a digital key pair that lets your laptop log in securely), (3) rent a GPU computer ("VM" = virtual machine) on Hyperstack, (4) connect to it three ways: PowerShell (a terminal), VS Code (an editor that works ON the VM) and WinSCP (drag-and-drop files), and (5) learn port forwarding, so web pages that run on the VM (Airflow, Gradio) open in your laptop's browser.
§ Word § Plain meaning
§ VM (virtual machine) § a computer in a data centre that you control over the internet
§ SSH § the secure way to log in to a Linux computer remotely; it uses port 22
§ SSH key pair § two files: the private key (stays on your laptop, never share) and the public key (.pub, you give it to Hyperstack)
§ public IP § the internet address of your VM, for example 38.80.123.45
§ environment (Hyperstack) § a folder in Hyperstack that holds your VMs and keys in one region
§ flavor (Hyperstack) § the hardware size: which GPU, how many CPUs and how much memory
§ image (OS image) § the operating system installed on the VM; ours already has the NVIDIA driver and Docker
§ port forwarding § a tunnel inside SSH: http://localhost:7860 on your laptop shows what runs on port 7860 of the VM
Result at the end of this part: VS Code on your laptop shows a terminal that says ubuntu@querymesh-gpu, nvidia-smi prints "NVIDIA RTX A6000", WinSCP shows /home/ubuntu, and a test web page on the VM opens in your laptop's browser.
## SECTION 2A.1 - Install VS Code on your laptop
WHERE: web browser on your Windows laptop
GOAL: VS Code installed.
Step 1. Open https://code.visualstudio.com/download
Step 2. Click the Windows button (User Installer, x64).
Step 3. Open the downloaded file VSCodeUserSetup-x64-....exe.
Step 4. Click I accept the agreement, then Next.
Step 5. Keep the install folder, click Next, then Next again.
Step 6. On "Select Additional Tasks" tick Add "Open with Code" action to Windows Explorer file context menu and Add to PATH.
Step 7. Click Next, then Install, then Finish.
You should see: VS Code opens with a Welcome tab.
CHECKPOINT: VS Code is open.
If not: if Windows SmartScreen blocks the installer, click More info > Run anyway (the file comes from Microsoft).
## SECTION 2A.2 - Install the Remote - SSH extension in VS Code
WHERE: VS Code on your laptop
GOAL: VS Code can open folders on the GPU computer.
Step 1. Press Ctrl+Shift+X (the Extensions panel opens on the left).
Step 2. In the search box type Remote - SSH
Step 3. Click the result named Remote - SSH whose publisher is Microsoft.
Step 4. Click Install.
You should see: a new "><" icon in the bottom-left corner of VS Code.
Watch out: install the one from Microsoft only; ignore look-alikes.
CHECKPOINT: the extension shows "Installed" (or a gear icon instead of the Install button) [43].
## SECTION 2A.3 - Hyperstack account and credit
WHERE: web browser
GOAL: a Hyperstack account with money on it.
Step 1. Open https://console.hyperstack.cloud
Step 2. Click Sign up (or Register) and create the account with your e-mail and a password.
Step 3. Open the confirmation e-mail and click the link in it.
Step 4. Log in to https://console.hyperstack.cloud
Step 5. In the left menu click Billing.
Step 6. Activate billing (add a payment card when asked).
Step 7. Add credit: US$20-30 is enough for the whole manual.
You should see: your credit balance on the Billing page.
Watch out: Hyperstack only lets you deploy after you have topped up your balance. If the balance goes below 0, Hyperstack hibernates your VMs automatically [3].
Watch out: only use addresses ending in hyperstack.cloud; look-alike websites exist.
CHECKPOINT: the Billing page shows your credit.
## SECTION 2A.4 - Create the SSH key on your laptop
WHERE: Windows laptop > Start menu > type PowerShell > click Windows PowerShell
GOAL: a private key file and a public key file in C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\.ssh
Step 1. Check that SSH is installed. Type and press Enter:
```
ssh -V
```
You should see: OpenSSH_for_Windows_9.x ...
Step 2. Create the key pair. Type and press Enter:
```
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\hyperstack_ed25519 -C "querymesh"
```
Step 3. When it asks "Enter passphrase (empty for no passphrase)", press Enter.
Step 4. When it asks "Enter same passphrase again", press Enter.
You should see: "Your identification has been saved in C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\.ssh\hyperstack_ed25519" and a small square picture of random characters.
Step 5. Print the PUBLIC key:
```
Get-Content $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\hyperstack_ed25519.pub
```
You should see: one line starting with ssh-ed25519 and ending with querymesh
Step 6. Select that whole line with the mouse and press Ctrl+C (copy). Keep PowerShell open.
§ Placeholder § Replace with § Example
§ YOUR_NAME § your Windows user folder name (shown in the "saved in" line) § rahul
§ Piece of the command § What it does
§ ssh-keygen § the program that makes key pairs
§ -t ed25519 § the key type: modern, short and secure
§ -f $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\hyperstack_ed25519 § where to save it: your user folder, sub-folder .ssh, file name hyperstack_ed25519
§ -C "querymesh" § a comment at the end of the public key, so you recognise it later
Watch out: the file WITHOUT .pub is your private key: never upload, e-mail or share it.
Watch out: if ssh-keygen says the file already exists, type n and press Enter, then use the existing key (skip to Step 5).
CHECKPOINT: the folder C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\.ssh contains hyperstack_ed25519 and hyperstack_ed25519.pub
If not: if "ssh is not recognized", open Settings > System > Optional features > View features > search OpenSSH Client > Install, close and reopen PowerShell, repeat Step 1.
## SECTION 2A.5 - Create an environment in Hyperstack
WHERE: web browser > https://console.hyperstack.cloud
GOAL: an environment named querymesh-env (holds your VM and key).
Step 1. In the left menu click Virtual Machines.
Step 2. Click Deploy New Virtual Machine (the deploy form opens).
Step 3. Scroll to the Environment section.
Step 4. Click Create New Environment.
Step 5. In the name field type querymesh-env
Step 6. Keep the suggested region and click Create (or Save).
You should see: querymesh-env selected in the Environment section.
Watch out: the VM and the SSH key must be in the SAME environment, or the key cannot be used [2].
CHECKPOINT: querymesh-env exists and is selected. Keep this form open for the next section.
## SECTION 2A.6 - Import your SSH key into Hyperstack
WHERE: Hyperstack console > the same Deploy New Virtual Machine form
GOAL: Hyperstack knows your public key.
Step 1. In the SSH key area click Create New SSH Key.
Step 2. Click the Import SSH key tab.
Step 3. In Environment select querymesh-env
Step 4. Click in the public key box and press Ctrl+V (the ssh-ed25519 ... line from Section 2A.4).
Step 5. In Name type querymesh-key
Step 6. Click Import.
You should see: querymesh-key selected as the SSH key in the form.
CHECKPOINT: querymesh-key is listed for querymesh-env.
If not: "invalid key" = you pasted the private key or only part of the line; repeat Section 2A.4 Steps 5-6.
## SECTION 2A.7 - Deploy the GPU machine (every field)
WHERE: Hyperstack console > Deploy New Virtual Machine form
GOAL: one GPU computer that is starting up.
Step 1. Fill the form one field at a time, exactly as in this table.
§ Field (in the form) § Value § Why
§ Flavor (GPU card) § RTX-A6000, GPU count 1 (flavor name n3-RTX-A6000x1) § 48 GB of GPU memory runs Qwen3-4B in vLLM with room to spare, for about US$0.50 per hour [1]
§ Environment § querymesh-env § holds your key
§ OS image (tab OS Images) § Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS R570 CUDA 12.8 with Docker § NVIDIA driver 570, CUDA 12.8, Docker and the NVIDIA container toolkit already installed; user ubuntu is in the docker group [4]
§ SSH key § querymesh-key § lets you log in
§ SSH Access § ON (toggle enabled) § opens port 22 in the firewall
§ Public IP Address § ON (toggle enabled) § you connect over the internet
§ Number of VMs to deploy § 1 § one machine
§ Virtual Machine Name (Additional settings) § querymesh-gpu § easy to recognise
§ Jupyter Notebook, Cloud-init Script, Labels, Firewall Rules § leave empty / off § not needed; we use port forwarding instead of opening ports
Step 2. Look at Final Configuration and Price Breakdown and check the hourly price (about US$0.50 per hour for 1x RTX A6000, verify).
Step 3. Click Deploy.
You should see: the Virtual Machines page with querymesh-gpu and a status such as CREATING or BUILD.
Watch out: billing starts when the status becomes ACTIVE [2].
§ If you see... § It means... § Do this next
§ RTX-A6000 is greyed out § sold out in this region § choose 1x L40 (n3-L40x1, about US$1.00/h) or 1x A100 80GB (n3-A100x1, about US$1.35/h); everything in this manual works the same
§ the image "Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS R570 CUDA 12.8 with Docker" is missing § different images in this region § choose "Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS R595 CUDA 13.2 with Docker" (newer driver, also works with the vLLM image)
§ "Insufficient balance" § no credit § go back to Section 2A.3 Step 7
CHECKPOINT: querymesh-gpu appears in the Virtual Machines list.
## SECTION 2A.8 - Wait for ACTIVE, copy the IP, check the SSH firewall
WHERE: Hyperstack console > Virtual Machines
GOAL: the VM is running and reachable on port 22.
Step 1. Wait until the Status column shows ACTIVE (usually 2-10 minutes; refresh the page).
Step 2. In the PUBLIC IP column, copy the address and paste it into secrets.txt as VM_IP = ...
Step 3. Click the VM name querymesh-gpu (the VM details page opens).
Step 4. Click the Firewall tab.
Step 5. Make sure Enable SSH access is ON.
Step 6. Under Inbound Rules, check there is a TCP port 22 rule with status SUCCESS.
Step 7. Click the Networking tab and find Connect over SSH: it shows a command like ssh ubuntu@38.80.123.45 (you add your key to it in the next section).
You should see: ACTIVE, a public IP, and the port 22 rule.
Watch out: do not add rules for 8000, 9200 or 8080. Those services have no password of their own or only an API key; you will reach them safely through SSH port forwarding (Section 2A.12) [5].
CHECKPOINT: you have VM_IP and the port 22 rule shows SUCCESS.
If not: no public IP = Networking tab > turn on Public IP Attached.
## SECTION 2A.9 - First connection from PowerShell
WHERE: Windows PowerShell on your laptop
GOAL: log in to the GPU computer and see the GPU.
Step 1. Type (replace VM_IP) and press Enter:
```
ssh -i $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\hyperstack_ed25519 ubuntu@VM_IP
```
Step 2. When asked "Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no/[fingerprint])?" type yes and press Enter.
You should see: a welcome text and the prompt ubuntu@querymesh-gpu:~$
Step 3. Type and press Enter:
```
nvidia-smi
```
You should see: a table containing "NVIDIA RTX A6000", "49140MiB" (48 GB) of memory, "Driver Version: 570.xx" and "CUDA Version: 12.8".
Step 4. Check the disk space:
```
df -h /
```
You should see: the root disk / with at least 80 GB free. The vLLM image (about 10 GB), the model (about 8 GB) and the Python packages (about 8 GB) fit easily.
Step 5. Type exit and press Enter (you are back on your laptop).
§ Placeholder § Replace with § Example
§ VM_IP § the PUBLIC IP from Section 2A.8 § 38.80.123.45
CHECKPOINT: nvidia-smi showed the RTX A6000 and driver 570 or newer.
If not: see Section 2A.14.
## SECTION 2A.10 - Connect VS Code to the GPU computer (Remote-SSH)
WHERE: VS Code on your laptop
GOAL: VS Code opens folders and terminals ON the GPU computer.
Step 1. Press F1 (a command box opens at the top).
Step 2. Type Remote-SSH: Open SSH Configuration File and press Enter.
Step 3. Click the first file: C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\.ssh\config
Step 4. Paste this at the end of the file (replace VM_IP and YOUR_NAME):
```
Host querymesh
    HostName VM_IP
    User ubuntu
    IdentityFile C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\.ssh\hyperstack_ed25519
    ServerAliveInterval 60
```
Step 5. Press Ctrl+S to save.
Step 6. Press F1.
Step 7. Type Remote-SSH: Connect to Host and press Enter.
Step 8. Click querymesh.
Step 9. If a box asks "Select the platform of the remote host", click Linux.
Step 10. Wait until the bottom-left corner shows SSH: querymesh (the first time VS Code installs a small server on the VM, about 1 minute).
Step 11. In the top menu click Terminal > New Terminal.
You should see: a terminal panel at the bottom with the prompt ubuntu@querymesh-gpu:~$
Step 12. In that terminal type hostname and press Enter.
You should see: querymesh-gpu
§ Line in the config § What it means
§ Host querymesh § a short nickname you choose
§ HostName VM_IP § the real address
§ User ubuntu § the login name on Hyperstack Ubuntu images
§ IdentityFile ... § which private key to use
§ ServerAliveInterval 60 § send a small "I am here" message every 60 seconds so the connection does not drop
Watch out: from now on, "VS Code Terminal" in this manual ALWAYS means this terminal connected to the GPU computer (bottom-left shows SSH: querymesh), not your laptop.
CHECKPOINT: hostname prints querymesh-gpu.
If not: F1 > Remote-SSH: Kill VS Code Server on Host > querymesh, then repeat Steps 6-10.
## SECTION 2A.11 - Install and connect WinSCP (drag-and-drop files)
WHERE: web browser, then WinSCP on your laptop
GOAL: see the GPU computer's files next to your laptop files, and open them in VS Code with a double-click.
Step 1. Open https://winscp.net/eng/download.php
Step 2. Click DOWNLOAD WINSCP (the installation package).
Step 3. Run the downloaded WinSCP-x.x.x-Setup.exe.
Step 4. Choose Install for me only.
Step 5. Accept the licence, choose Typical installation, then Commander interface.
Step 6. Click Install, then Finish.
Step 7. Open WinSCP. The Login window appears.
Step 8. Click New Site.
Step 9. Fill the fields:
§ Field § Value § Why
§ File protocol § SFTP § file transfer inside SSH
§ Host name § VM_IP § your GPU computer
§ Port number § 22 § SSH port
§ User name § ubuntu § the login name
§ Password § (empty) § you use a key
Step 10. Click Advanced...
Step 11. In the left list click SSH > Authentication.
Step 12. Next to Private key file click the ... button.
Step 13. In the file window change the file type filter (bottom right) to All Files (*.*).
Step 14. Open C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\.ssh\hyperstack_ed25519 (the file WITHOUT .pub).
Step 15. WinSCP asks to convert the key to PuTTY format: click OK, then Save (it creates hyperstack_ed25519.ppk).
Step 16. Click OK to close Advanced.
Step 17. Click Save, type querymesh as the site name, click OK.
Step 18. Click Login.
Step 19. If asked "Continue connecting to an unknown server?", click Accept.
You should see: two panels: your laptop on the left, /home/ubuntu on the right.
How to copy files: drag a file from the right panel to the left panel = download to your laptop; drag from left to right = upload to the GPU computer.
Now connect WinSCP to VS Code, so a double-click on a VM file opens it in VS Code:
Step 20. In WinSCP click Options > Preferences.
Step 21. In the left list click Editors.
Step 22. Click Add....
Step 23. Choose External editor and click Browse.
Step 24. Open C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\AppData\Local\Programs\Microsoft VS Code\Code.exe
Step 25. Click OK.
Step 26. Select the new Code.exe line and click Up until it is at the top of the list.
Step 27. Click OK.
You should see: double-clicking a text file in the right panel opens it in VS Code; when you save it (Ctrl+S), WinSCP uploads it back to the VM.
Watch out: for project files, prefer editing directly in the VS Code Remote-SSH window (Section 2A.10). Use the WinSCP + VS Code editor for quick looks and for copying files to and from your laptop.
CHECKPOINT: the right panel shows /home/ubuntu.
If not: "Server refused our key" = choose the .ppk file WinSCP created in Step 15 [43].
## SECTION 2A.12 - Port forwarding: open VM web pages in your laptop browser
WHERE: VS Code (connected, bottom-left shows SSH: querymesh)
GOAL: a web page served on the VM opens at http://localhost:PORT on your laptop, with no firewall change.
Step 1. In the VS Code Terminal start a tiny test web server on port 8765:
```
python3 -m http.server 8765
```
You should see: Serving HTTP on 0.0.0.0 port 8765 (http://0.0.0.0:8765/) ...
Step 2. In the bottom panel click the PORTS tab (next to TERMINAL).
Step 3. Click Forward a Port (or the + button).
Step 4. Type 8765 and press Enter.
You should see: a row with Port 8765 and Forwarded Address localhost:8765.
Step 5. On your laptop, open the browser at http://localhost:8765
You should see: "Directory listing for /" with the files of /home/ubuntu.
Step 6. Back in the VS Code Terminal, press Ctrl+C to stop the test server.
Step 7. In the PORTS tab, right-click the 8765 row and click Stop Forwarding Port.
The ports you will forward later:
§ Port § Service § Section
§ 8080 § Airflow web UI § 4.8
§ 8090 § QueryMesh API (/docs page) § 6A.7
§ 7860 § Gradio web chat § 6A.9
§ 8101-8105 § the five agents (Agent Cards) § 6A.3
Watch out: VS Code often forwards a port automatically when a program prints a localhost address; check the PORTS tab before adding it again. The forward only works while VS Code is connected.
CHECKPOINT: the directory listing opened in your laptop browser.
If not: if the browser says "connection refused", the server on the VM is not running (repeat Step 1) or the port number differs between Step 1 and Step 4.
## SECTION 2A.13 - Stop paying: hibernate, restore and delete
WHERE: Hyperstack console > Virtual Machines > click querymesh-gpu
GOAL: no GPU bill while you are not working (do this EVERY time you stop).
Step 1. In the VS Code Terminal, check nothing important is running: type tmux ls (you learn tmux in Part 2B; "no server running" is fine).
Step 2. In the Hyperstack console, open the VM details page (click querymesh-gpu).
Step 3. Hover over the menu with three dots in the top-right corner.
Step 4. Click Hibernate this VM.
Step 5. In the dialog, leave "Keep Public IP attached?" OFF (cheaper; you get a new IP later) or turn it ON (small hourly charge, same IP).
Step 6. Click Hibernate this VM to confirm.
You should see: status HIBERNATING, then HIBERNATED.
Step 7. To work again: open the VM details page, hover over the three-dot menu and click Restore (wait for ACTIVE).
Step 8. If the public IP changed: put the new IP in HostName of C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\.ssh\config (Section 2A.10 Steps 1-5) and in the WinSCP site (Edit > Host name).
§ VM status § GPU billed? § What is kept
§ ACTIVE § yes § everything
§ SHUTOFF (Stop) § YES, still billed [3] § everything
§ HIBERNATED § no (only the saved disk and a kept IP are billed) [3] § your files in /home/ubuntu, Docker images and volumes
§ Deleted § no § nothing
After Restore, the Docker containers vLLM and OpenSearch start again by themselves (they use --restart unless-stopped). FakeCloud, Airflow and the agents do not: restart them with the commands in Part 9 (QM Parts 8-14 - Reference).
Watch out: "Stop" is NOT a money saver on Hyperstack. Use Hibernate this VM or Delete.
Watch out: restoring needs the same GPU flavor to be in stock; if it is not, try later. Push your code to GitHub often (Section 2B.13), so you can rebuild on a new VM if needed.
CHECKPOINT: when you finish a work session, the VM shows HIBERNATED.
## SECTION 2A.14 - Connection troubleshooting
WHERE: PowerShell, VS Code or WinSCP on your laptop, and the Hyperstack console
GOAL: fix any connection problem in a few minutes.
Step 1. Find your symptom in the table and do the fix in the right column.
§ Symptom § Likely cause § Exact fix
§ ssh: connect to host ... port 22: Connection timed out § SSH access off, wrong IP, or VM not ACTIVE § 1) VM status must be ACTIVE. 2) Firewall tab > Enable SSH access ON. 3) Copy the IP again from the PUBLIC IP column
§ Permission denied (publickey) § wrong key file or wrong user § use -i with the file WITHOUT .pub and the user ubuntu; the key must be imported in the SAME environment as the VM
§ WARNING: REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED § new VM on an old IP § in PowerShell run `ssh-keygen -R VM_IP`, then connect again
§ VS Code stuck on "Setting up SSH host" § half-installed VS Code server § F1 > Remote-SSH: Kill VS Code Server on Host, connect again
§ WinSCP "Server refused our key" § key not in PuTTY format § use the .ppk file from Section 2A.11 Step 15
§ nvidia-smi: command not found § wrong OS image (no driver) § delete the VM and deploy again with an image whose name contains R570 CUDA 12.8 with Docker
§ localhost:PORT does not open in the browser § the service is not running or the port is not forwarded § check the PORTS tab; on the VM run `curl -s localhost:PORT` to see if the service answers
CHECKPOINT: after the fix, Section 2A.9 Step 1 logs you in again.
